    "content": "# Physical Chemistry Formulas \u2014 High-Yield Formula Sheet\n*Curated by Nelov Mallick (IIT Delhi) \u00b7 Authentic JEE Advanced Revision*\n\n---\n\n## 1. Thermodynamics & Thermochemistry\n\n### 1.1 State Functions & The First Law\n- **First Law of Thermodynamics:** $$\\Delta U = q + w$$ (IUPAC convention: work done on system is positive)\n- **Work in Reversible Isothermal Expansion of Ideal Gas:**\n  $$w_{\\text{rev}} = -\\int_{V_1}^{V_2} P_{\\text{ext}} dV = -nRT \\ln\\left(\\frac{V_2}{V_1}\\right) = -2.303 nRT \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{V_2}{V_1}\\right)$$\n- **Work in Irreversible Expansion against Constant $P_{\\text{ext}}$:**\n  $$w_{\\text{irrev}} = -P_{\\text{ext}}(V_2 - V_1)$$\n- **Reversible Adiabatic Process:**\n  $$P V^\\gamma = \\text{const}, \\quad T V^{\\gamma-1} = \\text{const}, \\quad T^\\gamma P^{1-\\gamma} = \\text{const}$$\n  $$w_{\\text{adia}} = \\Delta U = n C_v \\Delta T = \\frac{P_2 V_2 - P_1 V_1}{\\gamma - 1}$$\n- **Enthalpy & Internal Energy Relation:**\n  $$\\Delta H = \\Delta U + \\Delta n_g RT$$ ($\\Delta n_g = \\sum n_{\\text{gaseous products}} - \\sum n_{\\text{gaseous reactants}}$)\n\n### 1.2 Second & Third Laws, Entropy & Gibbs Free Energy\n- **Entropy Change:**\n  $$\\Delta S_{\\text{sys}} = \\int \\frac{dq_{\\text{rev}}}{T}$$\n  - For Ideal Gas: $$\\Delta S = n C_v \\ln\\left(\\frac{T_2}{T_1}\\right) + n R \\ln\\left(\\frac{V_2}{V_1}\\right)$$\n  - Isothermal: $$\\Delta S = n R \\ln\\left(\\frac{V_2}{V_1}\\right) = n R \\ln\\left(\\frac{P_1}{P_2}\\right)$$\n- **Gibbs Free Energy & Spontaneity:**\n  $$\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S$$\n  - $\\Delta G < 0$: Spontaneous process\n  - $\\Delta G = 0$: Equilibrium state\n  - $\\Delta G > 0$: Non-spontaneous process\n- **Standard Free Energy & Equilibrium Constant:**\n  $$\\Delta G^\\circ = -RT \\ln K_{\\text{eq}} = -2.303 RT \\log_{10} K_{\\text{eq}}$$\n- **Van 't Hoff Isochore Equation:**\n  $$\\frac{d\\ln K}{dT} = \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ}{RT^2} \\implies \\ln\\left(\\frac{K_2}{K_1}\\right) = \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ}{R}\\left(\\frac{1}{T_1} - \\frac{1}{T_2}\\right)$$\n\n---\n\n## 2. Chemical & Ionic Equilibrium\n\n### 2.1 Equilibrium Constants\n- **Relation between $K_p$ and $K_c$:**\n  $$K_p = K_c (RT)^{\\Delta n_g}$$\n- **Degree of Dissociation ($\\alpha$) from Vapor Density:**\n  $$\\alpha = \\frac{D - d}{(n - 1)d}$$ ($D$: theoretical vapor density, $d$: observed vapor density)\n\n### 2.2 Ionic Equilibrium & Buffers\n- **Ostwald's Dilution Law (Weak Electrolyte):**\n  $$K_a = \\frac{C\\alpha^2}{1 - \\alpha} \\approx C\\alpha^2 \\implies \\alpha = \\sqrt{\\frac{K_a}{C}}, \\quad [H^+] = C\\alpha = \\sqrt{K_a C}$$\n- **pH Definition:** $$\\text{pH} = -\\log_{10}[H_3O^+], \\quad \\text{pH} + \\text{pOH} = \\text{pK}_w = 14 \\text{ (at } 25^\\circ\\text{C)}$$\n- **Acidic Buffer (Weak Acid + Conjugate Salt):**\n  $$\\text{pH} = \\text{pK}_a + \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{[\\text{Salt}]}{[\\text{Acid}]}\\right)$$ (Henderson-Hasselbalch equation)\n- **Basic Buffer (Weak Base + Conjugate Salt):**\n  $$\\text{pOH} = \\text{pK}_b + \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{[\\text{Salt}]}{[\\text{Base}]}\\right) \\implies \\text{pH} = 14 - \\text{pOH}$$\n- **Hydrolysis of Salts:**\n  - Weak Acid + Strong Base: $$\\text{pH} = 7 + \\frac{1}{2}\\text{pK}_a + \\frac{1}{2}\\log C$$\n  - Strong Acid + Weak Base: $$\\text{pH} = 7 - \\frac{1}{2}\\text{pK}_b - \\frac{1}{2}\\log C$$\n  - Weak Acid + Weak Base: $$\\text{pH} = 7 + \\frac{1}{2}\\text{pK}_a - \\frac{1}{2}\\text{pK}_b$$ (independent of concentration)\n- **Solubility Product ($K_{sp}$):**\n  - For $A_x B_y \\rightleftharpoons x A^{y+} + y B^{x-}$:\n    $$K_{sp} = (xs)^x (ys)^y = x^x y^y s^{x+y}$$\n\n---\n\n## 3. Electrochemistry\n\n- **Nernst Equation for Half-cell & Full Cell:**\n  $$E_{\\text{cell}} = E^\\circ_{\\text{cell}} - \\frac{RT}{nF}\\ln Q = E^\\circ_{\\text{cell}} - \\frac{0.0591}{n}\\log_{10} Q \\quad (\\text{at } 298\\text{ K})$$\n- **Standard Cell Potential & Gibbs Free Energy:**\n  $$\\Delta G^\\circ = -n F E^\\circ_{\\text{cell}}$$\n- **Equilibrium Constant from $E^\\circ$:**\n  $$E^\\circ_{\\text{cell}} = \\frac{0.0591}{n}\\log_{10} K_{\\text{eq}}$$\n- **Electrolytic Conductance:**\n  $$\\kappa = \\frac{1}{\\rho} = G \\left(\\frac{\\ell}{A}\\right), \\quad \\Lambda_m = \\frac{\\kappa \\times 1000}{M}$$\n- **Kohlrausch's Law of Independent Migration:**\n  $$\\Lambda_m^\\circ = \\nu_+ \\lambda_+^\\circ + \\nu_- \\lambda_-^\\circ$$\n- **Faraday's Laws of Electrolysis:**\n  $$m = Z I t = \\left(\\frac{E}{96500}\\right) I t$$\n\n---\n\n## 4. Chemical Kinetics\n\n- **Differential Rate Law:**\n  $$-\\frac{1}{a}\\frac{d[A]}{dt} = k [A]^x [B]^y$$ ($n = x + y$: overall order)\n- **Zero Order Kinetics:**\n  $$[A]_t = [A]_0 - kt, \\quad t_{1/2} = \\frac{[A]_0}{2k}$$\n- **First Order Kinetics:**\n  $$[A]_t = [A]_0 e^{-kt} \\implies k = \\frac{2.303}{t}\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{[A]_0}{[A]_t}\\right)$$\n  $$t_{1/2} = \\frac{\\ln 2}{k} = \\frac{0.693}{k} \\text{ (independent of initial concentration)}$$\n- **Second Order Kinetics ($2A \\to \\text{Products}$):**\n  $$\\frac{1}{[A]_t} - \\frac{1}{[A]_0} = kt, \\quad t_{1/2} = \\frac{1}{k [A]_0}$$\n- **Arrhenius Equation & Activation Energy:**\n  $$k = A e^{-E_a / RT} \\implies \\ln k = \\ln A - \\frac{E_a}{RT}$$\n  $$\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{k_2}{k_1}\\right) = \\frac{E_a}{2.303 R}\\left(\\frac{T_2 - T_1}{T_1 T_2}\\right)$$\n"
