    "content": "# Organic Named Reactions & Reaction Mechanisms\n*Curated by Nelov Mallick (IIT Delhi) \u00b7 Authentic JEE Advanced Revision*\n\n---\n\n## 1. Carbonyl Condensation & Enolate Reactions\n\n### 1.1 Aldol Condensation\n- **Requirement:** Carbonyl compound possessing at least one $\\alpha$-hydrogen in the presence of dilute base ($\\text{NaOH}, \\text{Ba(OH)}_2$).\n- **Mechanism:**\n  1. Base abstracts $\\alpha$-H generating resonance-stabilized enolate ion.\n  2. Nucleophilic enolate attacks electrophilic carbonyl carbon of second molecule.\n  3. Proton transfer yields $\\beta$-hydroxy aldehyde/ketone (Aldol).\n  4. Heating causes $E1cB$ elimination of water to form conjugated $\\alpha,\\beta$-unsaturated carbonyl compound.\n- **Cross-Aldol:** One partner lacks $\\alpha$-H (e.g. benzaldehyde + acetaldehyde) to avoid multi-product mixtures.\n\n### 1.2 Cannizzaro Reaction\n- **Requirement:** Aldehydes with **zero** $\\alpha$-hydrogens (e.g., $\\text{HCHO}$, $\\text{Ph-CHO}$, $\\text{Me}_3\\text{C-CHO}$) in concentrated base ($50\\% \\text{ KOH}$).\n- **Outcome:** Disproportionation (self redox) producing one alcohol molecule and one carboxylate salt.\n- **Hydride Transfer:** Rate-determining step is the direct transfer of hydride ($\\text{H}^-$) from the dianion/monoanion intermediate to another carbonyl.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Electrophilic Aromatic Substitutions & Phenol Reactions\n\n### 2.1 Reimer-Tiemann Reaction\n- **Substrate:** Phenol with $\\text{CHCl}_3 + \\text{aq. KOH}$.\n- **Active Electrophile:** Dichlorocarbene $(:\\text{CCl}_2)$, an electron-deficient neutral intermediate generated via $\\alpha$-elimination.\n- **Product:** Salicylaldehyde ($o$-hydroxybenzaldehyde) as major product due to favorable intramolecular hydrogen bonding.\n\n### 2.2 Kolbe's Synthesis\n- **Substrate:** Sodium phenoxide with $\\text{CO}_2$ at $125^\\circ\\text{C}, 4\\text{--}7\\text{ atm}$, followed by acidification.\n- **Product:** Salicylic acid ($o$-hydroxybenzoic acid).\n\n### 2.3 Friedel-Crafts Alkylation & Acylation\n- **Alkylation:** $\\text{Ar-H} + \\text{R-Cl} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Anhyd. AlCl}_3} \\text{Ar-R} + \\text{HCl}$ (prone to carbocation rearrangement and polyalkylation).\n- **Acylation:** $\\text{Ar-H} + \\text{R-COCl} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Anhyd. AlCl}_3} \\text{Ar-COR}$ (no rearrangement, deactivating acyl group prevents polyacylation).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Nitrogen Derivatives & Amine Reactions\n\n### 3.1 Hoffmann Bromamide Degradation\n- **Substrate:** $1^\\circ$ Amide ($\\text{R-CONH}_2$) treated with $\\text{Br}_2 + 4\\text{NaOH}$.\n- **Key Intermediates:** $N$-bromoamide $\\to$ Isocyanate intermediate ($\\text{R-N=C=O}$) via alkyl group migration with **retention of configuration**.\n- **Product:** $1^\\circ$ Amine with **one fewer carbon atom** ($\\text{R-NH}_2$).\n\n### 3.2 Gabriel Phthalimide Synthesis\n- **Substrate:** Phthalimide $\\xrightarrow{\\text{KOH}} \\text{Potassium phthalimide} \\xrightarrow{\\text{R-X}} N\\text{-alkylphthalimide} \\xrightarrow{\\text{aq. NaOH / } \\text{N}_2\\text{H}_4} 1^\\circ \\text{ aliphatic amine}$.\n- **Limitation:** Cannot synthesize aromatic $1^\\circ$ amines because aryl halides do not undergo nucleophilic substitution ($S_N2$) with phthalimide anion.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Key Reducing & Oxidizing Reagents\n\n| Reagent | Primary Target | Transformed Product | Key JEE Traps |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **$\\text{LiAlH}_4$ (LAH)** | Esters, Acids, Amides, Carbonyls | Alcohols, Amines | Vigorous; reacts violently with water. Does not reduce isolated $C=C$. |\n| **$\\text{NaBH}_4$** | Aldehydes & Ketones | $1^\\circ$ & $2^\\circ$ Alcohols | Inert toward esters, carboxylic acids, and amides. |\n| **DIBAL-H ($-78^\\circ\\text{C}$)** | Esters, Nitriles | Aldehydes | Low temperature is crucial to prevent further reduction to alcohol. |\n| **PCC / PDC** | $1^\\circ$ Alcohol | Aldehyde | Anhydrous; stops selectively at aldehyde without over-oxidation to acid. |\n| **$\\text{O}_3 / \\text{Zn-H}_2\\text{O}$** | Alkenes | Carbonyl fragments | Reductive workup preserves aldehydes; oxidative ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2$) yields acids. |\n| **Clemmensen ($\\text{Zn-Hg / conc. HCl}$)** | Ketones / Aldehydes | Alkanes ($\\text{-CH}_2\\text{-}$) | Avoid for acid-sensitive substrates. |\n| **Wolff-Kishner ($\\text{NH}_2\\text{NH}_2 / \\text{KOH / glycol}$)** | Ketones / Aldehydes | Alkanes ($\\text{-CH}_2\\text{-}$) | Basic conditions; avoid for base-sensitive substrates. |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Organic Chemistry (POC) Distinguishing Tests\n\n1. **Lucas Test ($\\text{Anhyd. ZnCl}_2 + \\text{conc. HCl}$):**\n   - $3^\\circ$ alcohol: Immediate cloudiness/turbidity.\n   - $2^\\circ$ alcohol: Turbidity within 5 minutes.\n   - $1^\\circ$ alcohol: No turbidity at room temperature; requires heating.\n2. **Carbylamine Test ($\\text{CHCl}_3 + \\text{alc. KOH}$):**\n   - Distinctive for **$1^\\circ$ aliphatic and aromatic amines** only; generates foul-smelling isocyanides ($\\text{R-NC}$).\n3. **Iodoform Test ($\\text{I}_2 + \\text{NaOH}$):**\n   - Positive for compounds containing $\\text{CH}_3-\\text{C}=\\text{O}$ or $\\text{CH}_3-\\text{CH(OH)}-$ group. Yields yellow crystalline precipitate of $\\text{CHI}_3$ (m.p. $119^\\circ\\text{C}$).\n4. **Fehling's & Tollen's Tests:**\n   - **Tollen's Reagent:** Ammoniacal $\\text{AgNO}_3$. Reduced to silver mirror by all aldehydes (aliphatic and aromatic) and $\\alpha$-hydroxy ketones.\n   - **Fehling's Reagent:** Reduced to red $\\text{Cu}_2\\text{O}$ precipitate by **aliphatic aldehydes** only (aromatic aldehydes like benzaldehyde do NOT respond).\n"
