    "content": "# Inorganic Chemistry \u2014 NCERT Trends & Coordination Compounds\n*Curated by Nelov Mallick (IIT Delhi) \u00b7 Authentic JEE Advanced Revision*\n\n---\n\n## 1. Coordination Compounds & Crystal Field Theory (CFT)\n\n### 1.1 Octahedral Complexes ($\\Delta_o$)\n- In an octahedral field, $d$-orbitals split into two sets:\n  - Lower energy: Triply degenerate $t_{2g}$ ($d_{xy}, d_{yz}, d_{zx}$), lowered by $-0.4\\Delta_o$.\n  - Higher energy: Doubly degenerate $e_g$ ($d_{x^2-y^2}, d_{z^2}$), raised by $+0.6\\Delta_o$.\n- **Crystal Field Stabilization Energy (CFSE):**\n  $$\\text{CFSE} = (-0.4 n_{t_{2g}} + 0.6 n_{e_g})\\Delta_o + m P$$ ($P$ is pairing energy)\n- **High Spin vs Low Spin Criteria:**\n  - $\\Delta_o < P$: Weak field ligands $\\implies$ High spin (maximum unpaired electrons).\n  - $\\Delta_o > P$: Strong field ligands $\\implies$ Low spin (electrons pair up in $t_{2g}$).\n\n### 1.2 Tetrahedral Complexes ($\\Delta_t$)\n- Splitting is inverted: $e$ lower, $t_2$ higher.\n- Splitting magnitude:\n  $$\\Delta_t = \\frac{4}{9} \\Delta_o$$\n- Because $\\Delta_t$ is always substantially smaller than $P$, tetrahedral complexes are **virtually always high spin**.\n\n### 1.3 Spectrochemical Series (Increasing $\\Delta$)\n$$I^- < Br^- < S^{2-} < SCN^- < Cl^- < N_3^- < F^- < OH^- < C_2O_4^{2-} < H_2O < NCS^- < EDTA^{4-} < NH_3 < en < bpy < NO_2^- < PPh_3 < CN^- < CO$$\n\n### 1.4 Magnetic Moment (Spin-Only Formula)\n$$\\mu_s = \\sqrt{n(n + 2)} \\text{ BM}$$ ($n$ is number of unpaired electrons)\n- $n = 1 \\implies \\mu = 1.73 \\text{ BM}$\n- $n = 2 \\implies \\mu = 2.83 \\text{ BM}$\n- $n = 3 \\implies \\mu = 3.87 \\text{ BM}$\n- $n = 4 \\implies \\mu = 4.90 \\text{ BM}$\n- $n = 5 \\implies \\mu = 5.92 \\text{ BM}$\n\n---\n\n## 2. Isomerism in Coordination Compounds\n\n- **Hydrate / Solvate Isomerism:** $[Cr(H_2O)_6]Cl_3$ (violet) vs $[Cr(H_2O)_5Cl]Cl_2\\cdot H_2O$ (grey-green).\n- **Ionization Isomerism:** $[Co(NH_3)_5SO_4]Br$ (gives ppt with $\\text{AgNO}_3$) vs $[Co(NH_3)_5Br]SO_4$ (gives ppt with $\\text{BaCl}_2$).\n- **Linkage Isomerism:** Ambidentate ligands like $NO_2^-$ ($\\kappa N$ nitro vs $\\kappa O$ nitrito), $SCN^-$ (thiocyanato vs isothiocyanato).\n- **Coordination Isomerism:** $[Co(NH_3)_6][Cr(CN)_6]$ and $[Cr(NH_3)_6][Co(CN)_6]$.\n- **Optical Isomerism:**\n  - $[M(aa)_3]^{n+}$ (e.g., $[Co(en)_3]^{3+}$) is non-superimposable on its mirror image $\\implies$ chiral, optically active ($d$ and $l$ enantiomers).\n  - $cis-[M(aa)_2b_2]^{n+}$ (e.g., $cis-[Co(en)_2Cl_2]^+$) is optically active; *trans* isomer has a plane of symmetry and is optically inactive.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Molecular Orbital Theory (MOT) Trends\n\n### 3.1 Diatomic Molecules with $\\le 14$ Electrons ($B_2, C_2, N_2$)\nDue to $2s-2p$ mixing, $\\pi 2p_x = \\pi 2p_y$ lies lower than $\\sigma 2p_z$:\n$$\\sigma 1s < \\sigma^* 1s < \\sigma 2s < \\sigma^* 2s < (\\pi 2p_x = \\pi 2p_y) < \\sigma 2p_z < (\\pi^* 2p_x = \\pi^* 2p_y) < \\sigma^* 2p_z$$\n\n### 3.2 Diatomic Molecules with $> 14$ Electrons ($O_2, F_2$)\nNormal energy ordering without significant $s-p$ mixing:\n$$\\sigma 1s < \\sigma^* 1s < \\sigma 2s < \\sigma^* 2s < \\sigma 2p_z < (\\pi 2p_x = \\pi 2p_y) < (\\pi^* 2p_x = \\pi^* 2p_y) < \\sigma^* 2p_z$$\n\n### 3.3 Bond Order & Magnetic Nature Summary\n$$\\text{Bond Order} = \\frac{N_b - N_a}{2}$$\n- $O_2$ (16 electrons): Bond Order = 2.0, **Paramagnetic** (2 unpaired electrons in $\\pi^* 2p$).\n- $O_2^+$ (15 electrons): Bond Order = 2.5, Paramagnetic.\n- $O_2^-$ (17 electrons, superoxide): Bond Order = 1.5, Paramagnetic.\n- $O_2^{2-}$ (18 electrons, peroxide): Bond Order = 1.0, Diamagnetic.\n- $N_2$ (14 electrons): Bond Order = 3.0, Diamagnetic.\n- $C_2$ (12 electrons): Bond Order = 2.0, Diamagnetic (both bonds are purely $\\pi$-bonds).\n- $B_2$ (10 electrons): Bond Order = 1.0, Paramagnetic (unpaired electrons in degenerate $\\pi$-orbitals).\n\n---\n\n## 4. High-Yield NCERT Periodic Anomalies\n\n1. **First Ionization Enthalpy:**\n   - Group 2 > Group 13 ($Be > B$ due to stable fully filled $2s^2$ subshell; $Mg > Al$).\n   - Group 15 > Group 16 ($N > O$ due to half-filled stable $2p^3$ configuration; $P > S$).\n   - $5d$ series elements have higher $IE_1$ than $4d$ due to lanthanoid contraction ($Hf > Zr$, $Au > Ag$).\n2. **Electron Gain Enthalpy ($\\Delta_{eg}H$ negative magnitude):**\n   - Group 17: $Cl > F > Br > I$ (interelectronic repulsions in small $2p$ subshell of Fluorine make incoming electron less favored).\n   - Group 16: $S > Se > Te > Po > O$ (Oxygen has least negative $\\Delta_{eg}H$ in the group due to compact $2p$ size).\n"
